{
  "abstract": "Finite derivative budgets in quadratic interpolation leave local divisibility that is absent from the full weighted-jet index. We identify the one-variable residue-disk tail exactly with a universal fractional lattice of weighted divided antiderivatives. For power-of-$p$ budgets this identification stabilizes after an explicit finite cutoff; for general budgets the normalized index differs by at most an explicit logarithmic term. Tame characters give a finite block algorithm. A recursive monic-polynomial construction yields a finite quadratic lower bound with an explicit $O_p(N(\\log N)^2)$ error. Classical Newton coefficient extraction and rational minors give a finite upper bound. At $p=2$, the resulting lower and upper asymptotic coefficients are $(\\sqrt{105}-9)/24$ and $307/4320$; convergence and optimality remain open. A weighted Taylor algebra identifies the first saturation layer at every prime; an explicit coupled row explains why exact univariate transfer can be strict. We transfer the construction to the original biconjugate triangular support through exact rational-slope floor sums. At the existing $D=331$, $P=13,000,000,000$ contract, the new iteration adds more than $0.00007874706569$ to the arithmetic margin, and the total local gain exceeds $0.00930390205838$. This comparison alone remains negative against the complete baseline. Exact finite modules, algebraic identities, integer sums, and directed arithmetic are supplied as author controls. No new discriminant endpoint, census, or classification is claimed.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
